"Bestow an embryo, O fathers, / a boy wearing a lotus garland;

so that there may be a male here, / so bring it about, O steam-drinkers!"

With [these verses] the wife should eat the middle ball.

'Be united, O righteous ones, / most full of sweetness with the wave;

uniting milk with milk, / pleasant for the winning of wealth'—

with this [mantra] he pours the water used for rinsing the milking vessel into the pot.

“With the broom they found the altar, the earth; / that earth spread out for the earthly one;

It bears the embryo within the creatures; / from it the sacrifice is extended perpetually.”

With this mantra he sweeps the altar towards the east three times with the broom.

“Men, make this altar now, / acceptable to the gods, in the lap of Aditi;

All the gods have been pleased with this; / may they enter the sacrificer for increase of wealth.”

With this mantra he makes it depressed in the middle, sloping towards the north-east, smooth, and well made; with its shoulders it encompasses the āhavanīya fire, with its hips the gārhapatya fire; with earth brought from elsewhere for one desiring cattle.

'I send you forth to the ocean; / go to your own source;

May our heroes be unharmed; / let not my milk be poured away.'

With this [formula] he accompanies [the waters] that have been poured out.

"I unite you, your hearts; / let your mind be united;

let your breath be united; / let your bodies be united."

With this [mantra], he mixes the materials together.

'Being taken up, deliver me from evil, / which I have committed unknowingly and which knowingly;

whatever sin and evil has been done by day, / from all that, being taken up, release me!'

With this he carries it forward. In the morning [he says]: '...whatever [sin and evil has been done] by night...'

'I offer the immortal offering into the immortal [fire], / Agni into the earth, for the winning of immortality;

Through you, O Agni, I win my desire, / which Prajāpati first won. / Agni into Agni, svāhā!'

With this he deposits it in the evening; in the morning [he says]: 'Sūrya into Agni...'

Before the Sviṣṭakṛt offering, he offers six oblations of clarified butter:

"To him of a hundred weapons, of a hundred powers, / of a hundred aids, subduing the adversary;

May Indra lead us across every misfortune / through a hundred autumns!"

"Let these four paths, measuring out the space, / stretch between heaven and earth;

Whichever of them shall bring us imperishability, / to that one do ye deliver us, O All-Gods!"

"Spring, summer, the honey-rich rains, / autumn, and winter—may [they] establish [us] in well-being;

In the windless calm of these seasons of a hundred autumns, / may we be free from fear!"

"To the Saṃvatsara, to the Parivatsara, to the Idāvatsara, to the Anuvatsara, to the Udvatsara pay great homage!

In the goodwill of these worshipful ones / may we live long, unharmed!"

"This is the welfare of the Saṃvatsara, of the Parivatsara, of the Idāvatsara, of the Anuvatsara, of the Udvatsara;

May she, not being angered, convey us across; / through this day may I attain the [next] day!"

"May Agni consume first, / for he knows how the oblation [is];

May he who belongs to all peoples / make the plants auspicious for us!"—

with this [mantra] in the case of *śyāmāka* grain; in spring, in the case of bamboo barley.

Having concluded the full-moon rite on the same day,
[with the verses]:
"Cosmic order indeed is Parameṣṭhin, nothing surpasses cosmic order;
on cosmic order the ocean is placed; by the ocean the earth is made firm;
Agni with sharp radiance, heat has been approached by the uṣṇih;
the head is placed in heat, in the splendour of Vaiśvānara;
Turn it with cosmic order! Reverse it with truth! Follow it with heat!
Bring it near with auspiciousness! Bring it near with strength!"
with a three-striped porcupine quill and an iron razor he turns around the hair and shaves the beard.

Having brought the full-moon sacrifice to conclusion on the same day,

"That heat which rolled around, from the edge of the earth;

by that sacred formula of his I turn it away, so that he may live."

With: 'O Jātavedas, go to the gods with the omentum; for you were the first hotṛ;

Unite, O Agni, with the body of clarified butter; may the desires of the sacrificer be true. Svāhā'—

having recited this, when the vaṣaṭ-call is uttered, he offers the omentum.

'We have come here to the earth's place of sacrifice, / where all the gods found delight;

crossing over with ṛc, sāman, and yajus verses, / may we revel with prosperity of wealth and with food!'

With this verse, he occupies the sacrificial ground along with its roots.

'O Viṣṇu, you who are nearest to us, / grant us protection and glory;

for you the honey-dripping streams / milk out the imperishable spring.'

Having touched the vessels with this verse addressed to Viṣṇu, drawn up the cord, and bound the sacrificial post with a threefold [rope], he brings up the animal victims: a goat for Agni in the Agniṣṭoma, a second for Indra and Agni in the Ukthya, a ram for Indra as the third in the Ṣoḍaśin, and a ewe for Sarasvatī as the fourth in the Atirātra.

"Do not terrify me, O King; do not strike my heart with malice;

make [my] vigor virile for life, for splendor!"

Having consumed each [portion] with this [mantra], he mutters while touching the heart region.

"Let Agni, the unforsaken, go forth first among those worthy of sacrifice;

let the lord of settlement find me a settlement;

may we follow you who have shone forth!" —

with this the sacrificer, placing the sacrificial enclosure in front, comes home, comes [home].

"Mitra, speaking, causes men to strive; / Mitra sustains both the earth and heaven;

Mitra watches the settlements without blinking: / offer the oblation rich in clarified butter to Mitra!"—

with this formula he should offer the first libation; then, having milked another [cow], he should offer the agnihotra again.

"Everywhere your brilliant flashes, pure one, driven by the wind, / roam, O pure Agni;

crushing mightily, divine, roaming in groups of nine, / daringly breaking, they consume the forests."

"To you, O Agni, human clans pray, / the knower of the hotṛ's duties, the discriminator, the best bestower of treasures;

to him who is hidden in the secret cave, O fortunate one, visible to all, / loud-sounding, well-sacrificed, resting in clarified butter."

Having cut off from the eastern half of the middle [cake] and from the eastern half [of the other], [the adhvaryu commands]: “Recite the invitatory verse for Indra connected with Rathantara.”

“Towards you, O hero, we call aloud, / like unmilked milk cows;

The ruler of this moving world, who sees the sun, / the ruler, om.”

“Recite the invitatory verse for Indra connected with Bṛhat.”

“You indeed we invoke, / we, the singers, for the winning of the prize;

You, O Indra, the true lord, men [invoke] among the Vṛtras; / you, om!”

“Recite the offering verse for Indra connected with Rathantara.” / “We who sacrifice to Indra connected with Rathantara…”

“…with steed in the contests. / Towards you, O hero, we call aloud, / like unmilked milk cows;

The ruler of this moving world, who sees the sun, / the ruler, O Indra, of the stationary; vaṣaṭ!”

"Stay, O Maruts, the departing courser, / swift as thought, the bull, beautifully formed;

By whom this fierce [wind] goes forth released, / him do you, O Aśvins, invest for well-being."

With this, he accompanies the rain-bringing wind from the north with the mantra.

'May the divine waters be for our aid, / may they be for our drinking;

may they flow blessings and peace upon us.'

'May the waters of the desert be auspicious to us, / may those of the marshland be auspicious to us;

may the ocean waters be auspicious to us, / and may the well-waters be auspicious to us.'

'May Mitra be auspicious to us, may Varuṇa be auspicious, / may Aryaman be auspicious to us;

may Indra and Agni be auspicious to us, / may wide-striding Viṣṇu be auspicious to us.'

With these three verses, they purify themselves beyond the cowpen.

"Suck this honeyed breast of the waters, / swollen, Agni, in the midst of the flood;

Enjoy the spring, the honeyed wave; / enter the oceanic flood!"

—with this, he mutters the unyoking of the fire.

With: "Indra indeed the singers...", from the eastern half; with: "You are held by a support; for Indra accompanied by the Arka...", he draws it. With: "This is your womb; for Indra accompanied by the Arka...", he sets it down. With: "To you, O hero, we shout...", on the southern half; with: "You indeed we invoke...", on the northern half;

"Now may we accomplish these worlds, / Indra and all the gods;

and our sacrifice, our body, and our offspring / may Indra accomplish together with the Ādityas!"

"That indeed was the eldest in the worlds, / whence the fierce one of glittering valor was born;

as soon as born he crushes the enemies, / after whom all the helpers rejoice,"

Eight female servants carrying water-jars, wearing striking garments, two by two around the mārjālīya hearth,

"Cows alone are fragrant, / cows have the fragrance of bdellium;

cows are the mothers of clarified butter; / let them be numerous here!"

—thus they sing; stamping their feet with: "This is madhūka honey!", they circumambulate clockwise.

"The cows of Maṅgīra indeed / did not drink the water of the Gaṅgā;

they drank at the river Sarasvatī; / they plunged towards the east!"

"When the village folk and the slave of Māṅgīra / utter malicious words,

either the village prospers in security, / or the draft ox suffers while drawing [the plow]!"

"Here we float, / let the wooden pegs stir about here;

having swallowed the honey for you, / like costus in a dragnet!"

The first [layer has] one hundred and eighty-six; the second, seventy plus ten;

the third should have thirteen and one hundred, the wise say;

the fourth is said to be one hundred and one and three bricks;

and the highest layer has three hundred and fifty-six [bricks].

The prick between the middle and the two loops / is called the Gāyatra measure.

On half a puruṣa with an aratni, / the square is measured with it.

By the enlargement of the ends of the wings and tail, / with the Gāyatra [measure] and other arrows;

Of the chariot-wheel fire altar, / which was concisely stated by Viṣṇu:

Now of the creator placed [within], / the other three times outward, outward;

in whose circle are dissolved / seven and a half men by the wise;

With half a man one should draw the hub; / then it is within the range of the layer.

The rim is beyond the spokes / by a sixty-third, the axle-socket;

by one hundred and thirty, accompanied by twenty, / and by four and a half additional [units];

or one should measure with aṅgulas the middle; one should make the drawing with twenty.

The Prārohins, the Vaikalins, / the Plākṣis, the Kaumudādayas;

Hāryaśvi is the fifth of these. / These are the Red Parāśaras.

The Kāṇḍūśayins, the Vāhatayas, / the Jāṃjayas, the Maimanāyanas;

Gopāli is the fifth of these. / These are the Blue Parāśaras.

The Kārṣṇājinas, the Kapiśroḍhas, / the Kārkayas, the Śāntātapas;

Puṣkarasādi is the fifth of these. / These are the Black Parāśaras.

The Āviṣṭāyanas, the Vārṣṇāyanas, / the Śmāmeyas, and the Lokāyas;

Vaikṣi is the fifth of these. / These are the White Parāśaras.

The Khāllāyanas, the Vārṣāyaṇis, / the Pūrṇayas, the Bilvayūpayas;

Nārṇi is the fifth of these. / These are the Bright Parāśaras.

Green darbha grass is fit for solemn sacrifice; / yellow is fit for domestic sacrifice;

that with roots belongs to the deity of the fathers; / variegated grass belongs to the All-gods.

Measuring twelve finger-breadths, prepared with the sacred part of the hand sacred to the fathers,
